    <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive review of how large language models are transforming clinical practice, medical research, and healthcare delivery &mdash; along with the critical challenges that must be addressed.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="metrics-grid">
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">&gt;90%</div>
      <div class="metric-label">GPT-4 USMLE Score</div>
      <div class="metric-context">Exceeded the passing threshold on all three steps of the United States Medical Licensing Examination.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">500+</div>
      <div class="metric-label">PubMed Articles (2023)</div>
      <div class="metric-context">Over 500 peer-reviewed publications on LLMs in medicine published in 2023 alone.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card warning">
      <div class="metric-value">Up to 30%</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Hallucination Rate</div>
      <div class="metric-context">Models can generate fabricated medical references or inaccurate clinical information in up to 30% of outputs.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">$10.5B</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Global Health LLM Market</div>
      <div class="metric-context">The healthcare LLM market is projected to reach $10.5 billion by 2030, reflecting rapid global adoption.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="main-content">

    <!-- Left Column: Opportunities -->
    <div class="column">
      <div class="section">
        <h2>What Are LLMs and Why Do They Matter?</h2>
        <p>Large language models (LLMs) such as GPT-4, Med-PaLM 2, and LLaMA are poised to reshape clinical practice, biomedical research, and medical education. Trained on vast corpora of text data, they encode substantial medical knowledge. The pace of adoption has been extraordinary, shifting the paradigm from specialized predictive models to general-purpose medical reasoning engines.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="section">
        <h2>Performance Benchmarks</h2>
        <p>LLM performance on standardized medical examinations demonstrates remarkable capability in understanding and generating medical text. GPT-4 scored above 90% on the USMLE, substantially exceeding the passing threshold of ~60%. Med-PaLM 2 achieved 86.5% on the MedQA benchmark, approaching expert physician-level performance. However, while they encode substantial medical knowledge, their performance on real-world clinical scenarios remains less well characterized.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="section">
        <h2>Key Clinical &amp; Research Applications</h2>
        <table class="app-table">
          <tr>
            <th>Application Area</th>
            <th>Example Use Cases</th>
            <th>Current Maturity</th>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Clinical Documentation</td>
            <td>Automated note generation, discharge summaries</td>
            <td>Pilot deployments</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Diagnostic Support</td>
            <td>Differential diagnosis, radiology interpretation</td>
            <td>Research stage</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Patient Communication</td>
            <td>Answering patient questions, simplifying jargon</td>
            <td>Early adoption</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Medical Education</td>
            <td>Exam preparation, case-based learning, tutoring</td>
            <td>Active use</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Biomedical Research</td>
            <td>Literature synthesis, hypothesis generation</td>
            <td>Emerging</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Right Column: Risks & Limitations -->
    <div class="column">
      <div class="section risks">
        <h2>Risks, Limitations, and Ethical Concerns</h2>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">Despite impressive benchmark performance, the gap between theoretical capability and safe clinical deployment is significant. Critical concerns persist across five domains:</p>

        <ul class="risk-list">
          <li>
            <h3>1. Hallucinations</h3>
            <p>LLMs can fabricate plausible-sounding but incorrect medical information, including fake citations. Studies report hallucination rates of up to 30% in medical question-answering tasks, posing a direct threat to patient safety.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>2. Bias and Equity</h3>
            <p>Models may perpetuate or amplify historic biases present in training data, leading to disparities in care recommendations across demographic groups and exacerbating health inequalities.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>3. Privacy and Security</h3>
            <p>Patient data used in prompts may be transmitted to external servers, raising serious HIPAA and GDPR compliance concerns regarding the exposure of protected health information.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>4. Lack of Clinical Validation</h3>
            <p>Most LLM evaluations rely heavily on multiple-choice benchmarks rather than rigorous prospective clinical trials. Retrospective text generation does not equate to clinical efficacy.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>5. Over-reliance Risk</h3>
            <p>Clinicians may develop automation bias, accepting LLM outputs without sufficient critical appraisal, potentially leading to diagnostic errors or inappropriate treatment plans.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Bottom: Framework -->
  <div class="framework-section">
    <div>
      <h2>A Framework for Responsible Deployment</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 28px; line-height: 1.5; color: #D0D8E0; margin-top: 30px;">LLMs hold transformative potential for medicine, but their deployment must be guided by evidence, equity, and robust governance to ensure patient safety.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="framework-grid">
      <div class="framework-item">
        <span class="number">01</span>
        <h4>Rigorous Prospective Evaluation</h4>
        <p>Testing in diverse real-world clinical settings before deployment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="framework-item">
        <span class="number">02</span>
        <h4>Human-in-the-Loop Design</h4>
        <p>Ensuring active clinician oversight of all LLM-generated outputs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="framework-item">
        <span class="number">03</span>
        <h4>Transparent Reporting</h4>
        <p>Clear disclosure of model limitations, data sources, and failure modes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="framework-item">
        <span class="number">04</span>
        <h4>Equity Audits &amp; Regulation</h4>
        <p>Routine assessment of differential performance and alignment with FDA/WHO guidance.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

</body>
</html>
